--TEST--
`Core\Debug::dump`'s variadic asserted on both sides: no arguments writes nothing at all, and every arity above it writes exactly one record per argument, in argument order and agreeing byte for byte with `render` — spec § 16, `rule:errors/renderings` and `rule:errors/debug-dump`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `dump` is the one `Core` member whose parameter list is a variadic
// (`nvs_stdlib::debug`'s `CoreTy::Variadic`), and every case beside this one
// calls it with a fixed, non-empty argument list. The arity none of them asks
// about is the empty one — the arity only a variadic has. A `dump` that wrote
// a bare newline, a blank record or an empty container for no arguments would
// pass all of them, and would put a stray line into the diagnostic channel of
// every program that ever dumped a spread which happened to come out empty.
//
// Both sides, because either half alone reads plausibly: a `dump` that
// rendered only its first argument satisfies "nothing for none", and one that
// wrote a newline per call satisfies "one record per argument" at every arity
// but zero.
class Subjects {
    // Distinct per position, so a `dump` that rendered its arguments in the
    // wrong order, or rendered one of them twice, fails on the sequence rather
    // than only on the count.
    public static function at(int $n): string {
        return Core\Debug::render($n) as string;
    }
}

// The six records the four calls below owe, taken through the member that
// shares `dump`'s walk. Standard output holds them so that the expectation on
// standard error is the same six lines stated twice rather than one hand-typed
// belief about what a dump looks like.
array<string> $rendered = [
    Subjects::at(1),
    Subjects::at(2),
    Subjects::at(3),
    Subjects::at(4),
    Subjects::at(5),
    Subjects::at(6),
];

echo "records owed ", Core\Arr::count($rendered), "\n";

foreach ($rendered as string $one) {
    echo $one, "\n";
}

// The lower side. Nothing is handed over, so nothing is owed: this call must
// leave the diagnostic channel exactly as it found it, and the first line of
// the section below is the first record of the *next* call.
Core\Debug::dump();

// The upper side, over three arities. 1 + 2 + 3 arguments is six records, and
// the empty call above contributes none — so a stray line from it shifts every
// remaining line and the byte comparison fails on the first of them.
Core\Debug::dump(1);
Core\Debug::dump(2, 3);
Core\Debug::dump(4, 5, 6);

// The runner reads an error expectation as "this run must fail"
// (`nvs_test::Case::expects_failure`), and a dump writes nowhere but the
// diagnostic channel — so a program that exited zero could not state what it
// wrote there. The throw buys the section below; the last line of it is the
// tier-4 floor's own record for that throw (`rule:errors/log-write`), which lands on the
// same channel. A property of the harness, not of `Core\Debug`.
throw new RuntimeError("the case ends here, so its standard error can be asserted");
--EXPECT--
records owed 6
int(1)
int(2)
int(3)
int(4)
int(5)
int(6)
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
int(1)
int(2)
int(3)
int(4)
int(5)
int(6)
{"level":"error","msg":"the case ends here, so its standard error can be asserted","fields":{"class":"RuntimeError"},"nodes":[{"function":"<script>","file":"case.nvs","line":%d}]}
